{"questions":[{"question_id":"masR4Kqxbhmju2CT","paperTitle":"AIEEE 2010","year":2010,"examDate":2010,"subject":"chemistry","chapter":"coordination-compounds","chapterGroup":"inorganic-chemistry","topic":"isomerism-of-coordination-compounds","type":"mcq","marks":4,"negMarks":1,"permalink":"which-one-of-the-following-has-an-optical-isomer-en-eth-2010-marks-4-masr4kqxbhmju2ct.htm","content":"Which one of the following has an optical isomer ?<br>\n(en = ethylenediamine)","options":[{"identifier":"A","content":"[Zn (en) (NH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>2</sub>]<sup>2+</sup> "},{"identifier":"B","content":"[Co (en)<sub>3</sub>]<sup>3+</sup> "},{"identifier":"C","content":"[Co (H<sub>2</sub>O)<sub>4</sub> en]<sup>3+</sup> "},{"identifier":"D","content":"[Zn (en)<sub>2</sub>]<sup>2+</sup>"}],"correct_options":["B"],"explanation":"For a substance to be optical isomers following conditions should be fulfilled \n<br><br><mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.566ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"4.09ex\" height=\"2.262ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -750 1808 1000\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mo\"><use data-c=\"28\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-28\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(389,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D44E\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D44E\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(918,0)\"><use data-c=\"29\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-29\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mspace\" transform=\"translate(1307,0)\"></g><g data-mml-node=\"mspace\" transform=\"translate(1474,0)\"></g><g data-mml-node=\"mspace\" transform=\"translate(1641,0)\"></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> A coordination compound which can rotate the plane of polarized light is said to be optically active. \n<br><br><mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.566ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"3.864ex\" height=\"2.262ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -750 1708 1000\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mo\"><use data-c=\"28\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-28\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(389,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D44F\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D44F\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(818,0)\"><use data-c=\"29\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-29\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mspace\" transform=\"translate(1207,0)\"></g><g data-mml-node=\"mspace\" transform=\"translate(1374,0)\"></g><g data-mml-node=\"mspace\" transform=\"translate(1541,0)\"></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> When the coordination compounds have same formula but differ in their abilities to rotate directions of the plane of polarized light are said to exhibit optical isomerism and the molecules are optical isomers. The optical isomers are pair of molecules which are non-super-imposable mirror images of each other.\n<br><br><mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.566ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"3.873ex\" height=\"2.262ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -750 1712 1000\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mo\"><use data-c=\"28\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-28\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(389,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D450\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D450\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(822,0)\"><use data-c=\"29\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-29\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mspace\" transform=\"translate(1211,0)\"></g><g data-mml-node=\"mspace\" transform=\"translate(1378,0)\"></g><g data-mml-node=\"mspace\" transform=\"translate(1545,0)\"></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> This is due to the absence of elements of symmetry in the complex. \n<br><br><mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.566ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"4.077ex\" height=\"2.262ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -750 1802 1000\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mo\"><use data-c=\"28\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-28\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(389,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D451\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D451\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(912,0)\"><use data-c=\"29\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-29\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mspace\" transform=\"translate(1301,0)\"></g><g data-mml-node=\"mspace\" transform=\"translate(1468,0)\"></g><g data-mml-node=\"mspace\" transform=\"translate(1635,0)\"></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> Optical isomerism is expected in tetrahedral complexes of the type <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.025ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"7.337ex\" height=\"1.595ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -694 3243 705\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D440\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D440\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(1051,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D44E\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D44E\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(1580,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D44F\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D44F\"></u